{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh's direction, 2026-10-05, on the demo story and the lane forms: the demo must tell its story the way a GitHub user knows it. A user opens an issue; other people comment on it; it may be assigned or self-assigned, optionally; a developer links a pull request that claims to close it; comments appear on the issue or the pull request and reference each other; the merge closes the issue. Labels, issue types, fields, projects and milestones are out of scope for now. Our lane model is more flexible in some ways and more constrained in others; the demo aligns towards the GitHub experience (generic comments accepted; assignment and self-assignment optional; terms, offers and acceptances not required by default) while showing the rules and constraints that improve the workflow, the split and merge of concerns, and the tracking of every step. The unified lane, which fills the gap between issue and pull request, must be part of the story and must be compelling as an incremental improvement over the current GitHub process. The rework is not needed until after I3 lands; the lane forms successor and the demo contract's follow-through carry it. The planner's proposed defaults, for discussion with hugh: assignment optional and never required for a merge; commenting open to any member of the membership scope; a pull request's closing link auto-closes the issue on merge with the link recorded, requester acceptance available as a rule, not a default; offered terms, holds and obligations shown only where a rule or a participant uses them.","body":{"direction":"demo-story-github-familiar","out_of_scope_now":"labels, issue types, fields, projects, milestones","source":"hugh, 2026-10-05 14:50 Eastern, feedback on the 15:00 sprint report","when":"after I3 lands; carried by the lane forms successor and the demo contract follow-through"}}